    deck: "Traps for English speakers",
    body: `
<p>English has one word for "not" and builds the rest with helpers: don't, doesn't, didn't, won't, haven't. Chinese has no helpers. It has two words for "not", and you choose by meaning: <b>不</b> <i>bù</i> and <b>没</b> <i>méi</i>. Both go right before the verb or adjective, and the verb itself never changes.</p>
<h3>不: not now, not usually, not going to</h3>
<p>Use <b>不</b> for habits, likes, states and plans: what you don't do, aren't, or won't do. English needs don't, isn't or won't; Chinese uses <b>不</b> for all three.</p>
{{ex:我不喝酒。|Wǒ bù hē jiǔ.|I don't drink (alcohol).}}
{{ex:她不忙。|Tā bù máng.|She isn't busy.}}
{{ex:我明天不去。|Wǒ míngtiān bú qù.|I'm not going tomorrow.}}
<p>Remember the tone change from earlier: <b>不</b> is said with the second tone before a fourth tone, as in <b>不是</b> <i>bú shì</i> and <b>不去</b> <i>bú qù</i>. Before the other tones it keeps its fourth tone: <b>不喝</b> <i>bù hē</i>, <b>不忙</b> <i>bù máng</i>.</p>
<h3>没 with 有: don't have</h3>
<p><b>有</b> <i>yǒu</i> (to have) is the one verb that never takes <b>不</b>. Its "not" is always <b>没</b>: <b>没有</b> <i>méiyǒu</i>. In speech the <b>有</b> can drop when a noun follows.</p>
{{ex:我没有钱。|Wǒ méiyǒu qián.|I don't have any money.}}
{{ex:我没钱。|Wǒ méi qián.|I don't have money. (casual)}}
<h3>没: didn't, haven't</h3>
<p>For an action that did not happen, use <b>没</b> (or <b>没有</b>) before the verb. English marks the past with didn't; Chinese marks nothing else. The time word, such as <b>昨天</b> <i>zuótiān</i> (yesterday), comes before the verb too.</p>
{{ex:我昨天没去。|Wǒ zuótiān méi qù.|I didn't go yesterday.}}
{{ex:他没吃饭。|Tā méi chīfàn.|He didn't eat. / He hasn't eaten.}}
<p>Later you will meet <b>了</b>, a word that often marks a finished action. It does not go with <b>没</b> + verb: the action did not happen, so there is nothing to finish.</p>
<h3>How to choose</h3>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>English</th><th>Chinese</th></tr><tr><td>I don't go (usually) / I won't go</td><td class="k">我不去。</td></tr><tr><td>I didn't go / I haven't gone</td><td class="k">我没去。</td></tr><tr><td>I'm not busy</td><td class="k">我不忙。</td></tr><tr><td>I don't have it</td><td class="k">我没有。</td></tr></table></div>
<p>A quick test: if English says <i>didn't</i> or <i>don't have</i>, reach for <b>没</b>. If it says <i>don't</i>, <i>isn't</i> or <i>won't</i>, reach for <b>不</b>. Saying no to <b>是</b> is always <b>不是</b>.</p>
{{ex:我不是老师。|Wǒ bú shì lǎoshī.|I'm not a teacher.}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> putting <b>不</b> in front of <b>有</b>, because English says "don't have". <b>我不有钱。</b> → <b>我没有钱。</b> And no 了 after 没: <b>我没去了。</b> → <b>我没去。</b></div>
`,
