    title: "的: possession and describing",
    blurb: "Say whose something is and what it is like, with the owner or the describer before the noun.",
    deck: "Describing things",
    body: `
<p>English can put the owner or the description before the noun ("my teacher's book", "a red car") or after it ("the book of my teacher", "a car that is red"). Chinese has only one place: everything that describes a noun goes before it. The little word that joins them is <b>的</b> <i>de</i>.</p>
<h3>Owner + 的 + thing</h3>
<p><b>的</b> works like English 's. The owner comes first, then <b>的</b>, then the thing. A pronoun works the same way: <b>我的</b> <i>wǒ de</i> is "my", <b>你的</b> <i>nǐ de</i> is "your".</p>
{{ex:这是我的书。|Zhè shì wǒ de shū.|This is my book.}}
{{ex:老师的手机很贵。|Lǎoshī de shǒujī hěn guì.|The teacher's phone is expensive.}}
<p>You can stack owners, biggest first, just as in English: <b>我老师的书</b> <i>wǒ lǎoshī de shū</i> is "my teacher's book". Never "the book of my teacher": there is no "of" word to put after the noun.</p>
<h3>Describer + 的 + thing</h3>
<p>The same <b>的</b> joins a description to a noun. In English, "a car that is very expensive" puts the description after the noun; in Chinese it must move in front.</p>
{{ex:我喜欢红色的车。|Wǒ xǐhuan hóngsè de chē.|I like red cars.}}
{{ex:这是很贵的衣服。|Zhè shì hěn guì de yīfu.|These are very expensive clothes.}}
<p>Color words ending in <b>色</b> usually take <b>的</b> too: <b>白色的狗</b> <i>báisè de gǒu</i> (a white dog). You can also drop the <b>色</b>: <b>白狗</b> <i>bái gǒu</i>.</p>
<p>When an adjective has <b>很</b> in front of it, <b>的</b> is needed: <b>很贵的衣服</b>, never <b>很贵衣服</b>. A short one-syllable adjective often joins the noun directly: <b>好朋友</b> <i>hǎo péngyou</i> (a good friend).</p>
{{ex:他是我的好朋友。|Tā shì wǒ de hǎo péngyou.|He's my good friend.}}
<h3>When 的 drops</h3>
<p>With family and close relationships, Chinese usually leaves <b>的</b> out after a pronoun: <b>我妈妈</b> <i>wǒ māma</i>, <b>我朋友</b>. Both <b>我朋友</b> and <b>我的朋友</b> are fine. Some pairs are set phrases with no <b>的</b> at all: <b>中国菜</b> <i>Zhōngguó cài</i> (Chinese food), <b>中文老师</b> <i>Zhōngwén lǎoshī</i> (a Chinese teacher).</p>
{{ex:我妈妈是老师。|Wǒ māma shì lǎoshī.|My mom is a teacher.}}
{{ex:她是我朋友。|Tā shì wǒ péngyou.|She's my friend.}}
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>English</th><th>Chinese</th></tr><tr><td>my friend</td><td class="k">我的朋友 / 我朋友</td></tr><tr><td>the teacher's phone</td><td class="k">老师的手机</td></tr><tr><td>a very expensive car</td><td class="k">很贵的车</td></tr><tr><td>a good friend</td><td class="k">好朋友</td></tr></table></div>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> keeping the English order and putting the owner after the noun. <b>朋友我的</b> → <b>我的朋友</b>. Whatever describes the noun goes in front of it.</div>
`,
