    deck: "Measure words",
    body: `
<p>English counts most nouns directly: three books, this ticket. It needs an extra word only for some things: three <i>cups</i> of tea, a <i>pair</i> of shoes. Chinese needs that extra word every time a noun is counted or pointed at. It is called a measure word (or "classifier").</p>
<h3>Number + measure word + noun</h3>
<p>The measure word sits between the number and the noun. Leave it out and the phrase is simply wrong: <b>三书</b> is not Chinese.</p>
{{ex:三本书|sān běn shū|three books}}
{{ex:两杯茶|liǎng bēi chá|two cups of tea}}
<p>Remember: two before a measure word is <b>两</b>, and <b>一</b> changes its tone to match the next syllable: <b>一本</b> <i>yì běn</i>, <b>一个</b> <i>yí ge</i>.</p>
<h3>这 and 那 need one too</h3>
<p>"This" is <b>这</b> <i>zhè</i> and "that" is <b>那</b> <i>nà</i>. Before a noun, they also take a measure word. So do <b>哪</b> <i>nǎ</i> (which) and <b>几</b> (how many).</p>
{{ex:这本书很好。|Zhè běn shū hěn hǎo.|This book is good.}}
{{ex:这张票多少钱？|Zhè zhāng piào duōshao qián?|How much is this ticket?}}
<h3>Which measure word?</h3>
<p>Each noun has its own, often linked to its shape. Learn each noun together with its measure word, the way the cards show it.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Measure word</th><th>Used for</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td class="k">个</td><td>people and many everyday things</td><td class="k">一个人</td></tr><tr><td class="k">本</td><td>books, notebooks</td><td class="k">三本书</td></tr><tr><td class="k">杯</td><td>a cup or glass of a drink</td><td class="k">一杯咖啡</td></tr><tr><td class="k">张</td><td>flat things: tickets, paper, tables</td><td class="k">一张票</td></tr><tr><td class="k">件</td><td>tops, coats, most clothes</td><td class="k">一件衣服</td></tr><tr><td class="k">只</td><td>most animals</td><td class="k">一只猫</td></tr><tr><td class="k">双</td><td>pairs</td><td class="k">一双鞋</td></tr><tr><td class="k">瓶 / 碗</td><td>a bottle / a bowl of something</td><td class="k">一瓶水，一碗面条</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:一只猫|yì zhī māo|a cat}}
{{ex:那件衣服很贵。|Nà jiàn yīfu hěn guì.|That piece of clothing is expensive.}}
<p><b>个</b> is the most common measure word, and it is right for people and many objects. But it is not a safe choice for everything: books, cups of tea and tickets sound wrong with it.</p>
<h3>这个 and 那个 on their own</h3>
<p>In a shop you can point and say <b>这个</b> <i>zhège</i> (this one) or <b>那个</b> <i>nàge</i> (that one), with no noun at all.</p>
{{ex:你喜欢哪个？|Nǐ xǐhuan nǎge?|Which one do you like?}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> leaving out the measure word, as English does. <b>这书</b> → <b>这本书</b>; <b>三书</b> → <b>三本书</b>.</div>
`,
