    body: `
<p>English uses "is" plus a small word for location: the bank <i>is next to</i> the supermarket. Chinese uses one verb, <b>在</b> <i>zài</i> (to be at), and puts the location word after the place.</p>
<h3>Thing + 在 + place</h3>
<p>To say where something or someone is, the thing comes first, then <b>在</b>, then the place. No <b>是</b> is needed. To ask, put <b>哪儿</b> <i>nǎr</i> (where) in the place slot.</p>
{{ex:银行在哪儿？|Yínháng zài nǎr?|Where's the bank?}}
{{ex:我在家。|Wǒ zài jiā.|I'm at home.}}
<h3>Location words come after the noun</h3>
<p>English puts "on", "in" and "next to" before the noun. Chinese puts them after it: "table on", "room in". These are location words.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>English</th><th>Chinese</th></tr><tr><td>on the table</td><td class="k">桌子上</td></tr><tr><td>in the room</td><td class="k">房间里</td></tr><tr><td>next to the supermarket</td><td class="k">超市旁边</td></tr><tr><td>across from the supermarket</td><td class="k">超市对面</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:书在桌子上。|Shū zài zhuōzi shàng.|The book is on the table.}}
{{ex:手机在房间里。|Shǒujī zài fángjiān lǐ.|The phone is in the room.}}
{{ex:银行在超市旁边。|Yínháng zài chāoshì pángbiān.|The bank is next to the supermarket.}}
<p>An object such as a table or a bag needs its location word to become a place: <b>在桌子</b> alone is wrong. Cities and countries never take one, and places such as a bank or a supermarket usually don't either.</p>
{{ex:我在北京。|Wǒ zài Běijīng.|I'm in Beijing.}}
<h3>在 + place before the verb</h3>
<p>To say where you do something, English adds "at a bank" after the verb. Chinese puts <b>在</b> + place before the verb, just as it does with time words. The order is who, then where, then what they do.</p>
{{ex:我在银行工作。|Wǒ zài yínháng gōngzuò.|I work at a bank.}}
{{ex:他在家吃饭。|Tā zài jiā chīfàn.|He eats at home.}}
<h3>在 or 有?</h3>
<p>In the lesson on 有 you met the other order. <b>在</b> starts with the thing you are talking about and says where it is. <b>有</b> starts with the place and says what is there.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Known thing: where is it?</th><th>Place: what is there?</th></tr><tr><td class="k">银行在超市对面。</td><td class="k">附近有银行。</td></tr><tr><td>The bank is across from the supermarket.</td><td>There's a bank nearby.</td></tr></table></div>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> putting the place after the verb, as in English. <b>我工作在银行。</b> → <b>我在银行工作。</b></div>
`,
